==English==
{{was wotd|2007|April|30}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|NL.|en}} {{m|la|*differentiatus}}, past participle of {{m|la|differentiare}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|differentia||difference}}; see [[difference]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/dɪf.əˈɹɛn.ʃi.eɪt/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˌdɪ.fəˈɹɛnt.ʃi.eɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-differentiate.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-differentiate.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[show]], or be the [[distinction]] between two things.
#* '''1871''', {{w|John Earle (professor)|John Earle}}, ''The Philology of the English Tongue''
#*: The word "then" was '''differentiated''' into the two forms "then" and "than".
#* {{RQ:Orwell Down and Out|passage=The mass of the rich and poor are '''differentiated''' by their incomes and nothing else, and the average millionaire is only the average dishwasher dressed in a new suit.|chapter=22}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[perceive]] the [[difference]] between things; to [[discriminate]].
#* {{quote-book|en|title={{w|New York Times v. Sullivan}}| year=1964| passage=he refused to instruct that actual intent to harm or recklessness had to be found before punitive damages could be awarded, or that a verdict for respondent should '''differentiate''' between compensatory and punitive damages.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive}} To [[modify]], or be [[modified]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|mathematics}} To [[calculate]] the [[derivative]] of a [[function]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|mathematics}} To calculate the [[differential]] of a function of multiple [[variable]]s.
# {{lb|en|intransitive|biology}} To produce distinct [[organ]]s or to achieve specific functions by a process of [[development]] called [[differentiation]].
#* {{quote-book|en|title=[[S:Insects, Their Ways and Means of Living|Insects: Their Ways and Means of Living]]|year=1930|author=Robert Evans Snodgrass| passage=In Chapter IV we learned that every animal consists of a body, or soma, formed of cells that are '''differentiated''' from the germ cells usually at an early stage of development.}}

